WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.410
In this video, we're going to look at how
to communicate more clearly this one's

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:07.100
really important. so let's get into it.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:15.440
I'd like to start with a paraphrase from
Aristotle. Clear speaking demonstrates

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:19.529
clear thinking. And that's the topic of
this video,

00:00:19.529 --> 00:00:23.699
clear communication, mainly through a
three-part framework that I will be

00:00:23.699 --> 00:00:28.170
showing you. And the main idea of this
whole video is that this is the way

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:32.850
leaders communicate. they almost all
communicate in this three-part framework.

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:37.120
And I'd like to give you three tips to
help you move in that direction.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:40.920
 

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:44.520
The first tip sounds easy but it takes a
little work to get there and that is to

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:50.100
figure out what your main idea is. If
you're writing a college essay this is

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:54.000
called your thesis statement. And as you
probably know, it takes a little bit of

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.559
work and reflection and a lot of
crossing out rewriting things to figure

00:00:58.559 --> 00:01:04.739
out how to say your main point in about
one crisp sentence. That takes some work

00:01:04.739 --> 00:01:09.210
but the extra effort it takes to get
there will help you communicate much

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:14.130
more clearly. In any kind of meeting
situation, conversation, presentation,

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:19.180
email, voicemail, you have to figure out
what your bottom line is.

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:23.340
Tip number two.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:27.140
Once you have figured out what your
bottom line is, you want to break that

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:32.579
bottom line down into three main points
or three buckets. You want to figure out

00:01:32.579 --> 00:01:37.020
what you want to say first, second, and
third. And once you figure that out you

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:41.040
have a basic structure that you can
communicate and offer. Now, if you're in a

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:46.320
longer presentation or a meeting you
have to preview these ideas and then

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:49.920
give some detail. If it's a short
conversation or an email, you can just

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:53.700
share them by listing them one at a time.
So let's just assume for a second you've

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:57.210
given your main point and then you've
previewed your three main points across.

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:00.360
You've given a little roadmap. First,
Second, Third. The next thing you want to

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:05.219
do is circle back around to the first
one and signpost. Remind people that

00:02:05.219 --> 00:02:08.580
you're talking about your first point.
Once you give all the details for that

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:12.770
first point, you go to your second point
and you say secondly and then you should

00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:17.180
your second point details. And once
you're done with that, you say third and

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:21.410
then you give your details under your
third main point. If you only give your

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:26.240
thesis and your preview statement and you don't then remind people by signposting

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:30.800
each of the main points, it's easy to get
lost in all the details of those main

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:34.010
points. It's very important if you
want to communicate clearly to give

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:36.830
those reminders there are called
signpost. Little points along the way

00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:40.430
where you remind people just in case
they got distracted, now I'm talking

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:44.240
about my second point and finally my
third point. Now I often get the question

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:49.130
why three main points? what if I have ten
things that I need to share. Well if

00:02:49.130 --> 00:02:52.340
you're still stuck with a whole bunch of
items like eight nine or ten items,

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:57.980
chances are you haven't taken the time
to step back a level and think about how

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:01.760
you might sort these into three main
points. We're going to remember three.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:05.630
We're not going to remember ten. So
usually if you look at a list of 10 or

00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:10.010
so items, you can create slightly larger
categories that you can bucket those

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:13.970
ideas into until you come up with around
three main points. Now on any given day,

00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:17.780
if you have two main points or four main
points, that probably doesn't matter. You

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:21.020
always want to shoot for three, though.
And that's a good discipline and habit

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:25.580
to establish. So, let's give some concrete
examples. A couple years ago, I lost about

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:29.720
50 pounds and naturally many people have
asked me how did you lose 50 pounds?

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:33.950
This is how I would answer my headline
was I had a three-part plan for losing

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:39.290
the weight. First, I counted calories. Next
I exercise more. And, third I stayed

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:44.000
motivated with a friend. If this were
a normal conversation, I would just say

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:47.630
it like that. But if we were having some
kind of meeting update, let's say, then I

00:03:47.630 --> 00:03:52.610
would circle back around give my first
main point again, the details. Signposts

00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:55.700
the second main point then give the
details. signpost the third main point

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:59.000
and then give the details. But this was
just a normal conversation so I don't go

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:02.000
into that. Let's give another example
let's say you're going to do an update

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:05.090
at a meeting and the question is,
how are things going in your [department]?

00:04:05.090 --> 00:04:09.650
Your headline would be, growth it's great
but it's causing strain. First, employees

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:13.489
are getting burned out. Second, it's been
hard to hire new people. And, third,

00:04:13.489 --> 00:04:18.799
quality is starting to slip. And again, if
this were a meeting with more details I

00:04:18.799 --> 00:04:21.710
would then add those details and give
the signpost. Now, let's say you're

00:04:21.710 --> 00:04:25.280
visiting a physician and you have
something wrong with your eyes and you

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:29.169
would say doctor what's
wrong with my eyes? And half after he or

00:04:29.169 --> 00:04:33.039
she examined you, the doctor might say
something like this. You have pinkeye.

00:04:33.039 --> 00:04:37.120
That's the headline. The causes are it's a
virus and it gets in there from touching

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:41.500
your eyes. Symptoms are it's red and get
itchy eyes as you can see. And the

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:45.729
treatment are these eye drops that I
would like to prescribe to you. so that's

00:04:45.729 --> 00:04:49.479
how this sounds when you give a nice
headline and three main points. It sounds

00:04:49.479 --> 00:04:53.770
extremely clear and the whole thing is
built for your listeners so that it'll

00:04:53.770 --> 00:05:01.780
stick. Now the third point I want to
share with you is a tip about using this

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:05.889
three-part framework not just for
communicating but for thinking and

00:05:05.889 --> 00:05:09.789
analyzing and listening. And here's what
I mean. This is a tool not just for

00:05:09.789 --> 00:05:15.310
expressing yourself but for processing
the communication that you're exposed to.

00:05:15.310 --> 00:05:17.979
So let's say you're listening and you're
in the flow of a

00:05:17.979 --> 00:05:22.659
meeting with some people at work or
among some friends, I recommend that you

00:05:22.659 --> 00:05:26.889
start taking notes and trying to figure
out what people's headlines are and what

00:05:26.889 --> 00:05:31.240
their buckets are. this is an extremely
valuable analytical skill that will help

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:35.259
you distill the messages that you're
hearing around you. You'll become an

00:05:35.259 --> 00:05:39.250
A+ listener for content almost
overnight when you start doing this.

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:43.990
This is not just a way to express
yourself like a leader and express

00:05:43.990 --> 00:05:48.460
yourself clearly, it's a tool that you
can use to analyze and think more

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:51.520
clearly and if you're doing it when
you're communicating and also when

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:55.840
you're processing information, you'll
really up your game and your skills in

00:05:55.840 --> 00:06:03.340
this area. So in sum, this three-part
framework will help you communicate much

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:07.690
more clearly. You'll communicate like a
leader and you'll start to think like a

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:12.159
leader as well. In terms of your homework, your action that like you take? The very

00:06:12.159 --> 00:06:17.710
next conversation, meeting, email,
voicemail, presentation--anything where

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:21.009
you know you're going to communicate, I
would like you to shape it into this

00:06:21.009 --> 00:06:26.860
headline and three main points and watch
this become a habit over time. And I'd

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:29.349
like to finish with a quotation by Paul
Meyer.

00:06:29.349 --> 00:06:36.039
He says communication is the key to
personal and career success. So thanks

00:06:36.039 --> 00:06:38.009
and I will see you in the next video.

